# Supabase Postgres Best Practices

When working on SQL queries, Supabase migrations, schema design, RLS policies, indexes, or database configurations, you MUST consult the Supabase Postgres best practices skill.

Chaque migration devra être ajoutée dans /supabase/migration

## Reference Categories (by priority)

| Priority | Category | When to consult | Prefix |
|----------|----------|-----------------|--------|
| 1 | Query Performance | Writing/optimizing SELECT, JOIN, WHERE clauses | `query-` |
| 2 | Connection Management | Pooling, timeouts, scaling | `conn-` |
| 3 | Security & RLS | RLS policies, GRANT/REVOKE, permissions | `security-` |
| 4 | Schema Design | CREATE TABLE, data types, primary keys, partitioning | `schema-` |
| 5 | Concurrency & Locking | Transactions, deadlocks, advisory locks | `lock-` |
| 6 | Data Access Patterns | Pagination, batch inserts, upserts, N+1 | `data-` |
| 7 | Monitoring & Diagnostics | EXPLAIN ANALYZE, pg_stat_statements, VACUUM | `monitor-` |
| 8 | Advanced Features | Full-text search, JSONB indexing | `advanced-` |

## Rules

- Read ONLY the reference files relevant to the current task (on-demand, not all at once).
- Apply the best practices from the references when writing or reviewing SQL.
- If a migration or query violates a best practice, flag it and suggest the corrected version.
- Prioritize CRITICAL categories (query performance, connections, security) over lower-priority ones.
